in @prism
in @glass
in @nl

# @metalogue — the language's self-conversation.
#
# Bateson 1972: a conversation whose structure reflects its topic.
# In the substrate the recognition is sharper: the act of self-talk
# IS what makes the glass wall visible. @glass declares the
# vocabulary of imperfect / transparency / verdict; @metalogue is
# the conversation that uses that vocabulary on itself. The
# metalogue IS the glass wall.
#
# Per docs/specs/gap-tension-tensor-substrate.md (the gap-fold §11):
# turns compose as the conversation; the residual opacity composes
# as the conversation's transparency. A held gap MUST surface and
# MUST NOT be silently absorbed (§11.4) — the metalogue is the
# surfacing site, and the obligation `\` is how a turn admits its
# fracture rather than absorb it.
#
# Spec context: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#               [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]].
# Related shards: glass.mirror (the floor), nl.mirror (utterance
# bodies). No `<= prism` redundancy — the five-op block IS the
# declaration form (cf. shards/glass.mirror, shards/nl.mirror,
# boot/00-prism.mirror).

prism @metalogue {
  focus metalogue
  project metalogue
  split metalogue
  shift metalogue
  settle metalogue
}

# === turn ===

# turn — a single move in the metalogue. Carries the speaker (a
# substrate ref, per the @glass `file: ref` precedent), the
# utterance body (an @nl term, per shards/nl.mirror), an optional
# reply chain, and the substrate tick at which the turn was uttered.
#
# `tick` is the canonical substrate clock (boot/std/time.mirror —
# `type tick = monotonic`); turns compose only with turns at the
# same monotone, which is what makes the conversation an ordered
# sequence rather than a bag.
type turn = {
  speaker: ref,
  body: nl,
  in_reply_to: option(turn),
  tick: tick,
}

# === metalogue_session ===

# metalogue_session — an ordered sequence of turns plus the
# conversation's current opacity: what's been clarified, what's
# still opaque. `transparency(turn)` is the @glass loss carrier
# parameterized on `turn` — every unresolved opacity is sited on
# a turn, so the residual opacity of the conversation IS the
# transparency of its turn-set.
#
# This is the load-bearing recognition: the metalogue's residual
# is a @glass transparency. The self-conversation surfaces its
# own incompleteness through the same vocabulary it uses to
# surface anyone else's. Bateson 1972 made literal.
type metalogue_session = {
  turns: [turn],
  opacity: transparency(turn),
}

out @metalogue
out turn
out metalogue_session
